# The SPLAT Field Guide, as published.
#
#   https://claude.ai/code/artifact/18992308-da75-4782-87b8-20b6ba7b4311
#
# The published page is a deterministic slice of docs/SPLAT-Field-Guide.html - that file minus the
# doctype/html/head/body wrapper and the two metas the artifact host supplies - so binding the source
# binds the page, and no network call is needed to check it.
#
# DocumentationDriftTests.ThePublishedFieldGuide_IsTheSourceAsItStandsToday recomputes this hash over
# the source with line endings normalised, and fails when the source has moved ahead of the live copy.
# That drift is not hypothetical: the page sat at 511 tests, telling readers that on Windows the locks
# "do not filter network sockets", for weeks after both repository copies had been corrected to say
# that 33 of 49 templates launch with no network capability at all. Every other claim in that class is
# checked against the code. That one was checked against nothing, because the only copy a non-engineer
# actually reads lives outside this repository.
#
# WHEN THE TEST GOES RED, both halves, in this order:
#
#     packaging/build-field-guide-artifact.ps1                    # writes the body to publish
#     ...publish that file to the URL above...
#     packaging/build-field-guide-artifact.ps1 -RecordPublished   # rewrites the hash below
#
# The switches are separate so that recording is a deliberate act. Updating the hash without
# publishing silences the alarm and leaves the page wrong, which is worse than no check at all,
# because the page then looks verified. That is the one failure this design cannot detect, and it is
# why the second command prints what it is asserting on your behalf.
#
# Do not hand-slice the wrapper any more. The script matches whole lines; a regex for `<head` also
# matches `<header class="hero">`, and an earlier republish lost that opening tag, so the live page
# rendered its title placard as bare text for months while every other copy looked right.

published  2026-09-23
source     docs/SPLAT-Field-Guide.html
